 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
 
-A language model may need mathematical reasoning, code generation, scientific knowledge, and instruction following within the same deployment. Multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) transfers such capabilities by having specialized teachers score responses generated by a shared student \citep{ma2026mopdmultiteacheronpolicydistillation}. Because the student supplies the responses, its current behavior determines both the examples on which teachers provide feedback and the lengths over which that feedback is averaged.
+Multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) is increasingly used in industrial LLM post-training to consolidate specialized capabilities into a single model. Recent examples include Xiaomi's MiMo-V2-Flash \citep{xiaomi2026mimov2flash}, NVIDIA's Nemotron 3 Ultra \citep{nvidia2026nemotron3ultra}, Kimi K3 \citep{kimiteam2026kimik3openfrontier}, and DeepSeek-V4 \citep{deepseekai2026deepseekv4highlyefficientmilliontoken}. In this paradigm, specialized teachers provide token-level feedback on responses generated by the student itself, allowing independently developed experts to transfer their capabilities to a shared student. This design supports capability integration across diverse domains while adapting the supervision to the student's evolving behavior.
 
-Equal numbers of prompts from different domains need not give those domains equal weight in the loss. Open-MOPD identifies response-length imbalance as a source of unequal supervision \citep{gao2026openmopddiagnosingfixingcapability}. This raises a more specific question about the objective: does an averaging rule balance domains, responses within each domain, or both? These choices can be separated algebraically and compared empirically through the capabilities of the resulting student.
+Recent work has advanced both the practice and analysis of this approach. \citet{ma2026mopdmultiteacheronpolicydistillation} demonstrate capability integration using policy-gradient and top-$k$ distillation losses. Open-MOPD identifies uneven capability transfer and develops mechanisms for balancing domain token shares, allocating supervision according to student--teacher gaps, and refreshing student-dependent rewards \citep{gao2026openmopddiagnosingfixingcapability}. The Nemotron 3 Ultra report further finds that top-$k$ and full-vocabulary matching do not outperform sampled-token supervision in its preliminary experiments \citep[Section~3.3.5]{nvidia2026nemotron3ultra}. Complementary studies characterize the sparsity and geometry of OPD parameter changes, including their concentration at checkpoint precision and their evolution during training \citep{yu2026denseupdates,shen2026opdgeometry}. These findings motivate examining how supervision choices translate into parameter changes and capability outcomes in a shared student.
 
-The parameter changes induced by teacher feedback raise a second question. Prior work finds concentrated changes in reinforcement learning \citep{mukherjee2025subnetworks} and distillation \citep{yu2026denseupdates,shen2026opdgeometry}. In a shared student, each teacher acts on parameters already influenced by other teachers. Concentration alone does not specify whether teachers affect the same locations. The relevant comparison therefore begins with a common student and optimizer state, and relates teacher-specific parameter changes to differences between teacher predictions.
+Several distinctions are central to this examination. Balancing domain weights still leaves open how responses of different lengths are weighted within each domain. Concentrated changes in stored model weights do not, by themselves, describe the breadth of optimizer updates. Likewise, supervising more vocabulary alternatives need not produce a proportional increase in changed parameters, and similar local update concentration may accompany different capability outcomes. We therefore ask: \emph{how do loss averaging and vocabulary supervision affect learning in MOPD, and what do parameter-change measurements reveal about these choices?}
 
-A third question concerns the breadth of teacher feedback. A sampled-token loss and a loss over several vocabulary alternatives supervise the same generated prefix differently. Their vocabulary coverage does not directly determine how many model parameters change: the softmax couples logits, and the optimizer combines the current gradient with its history. Comparing both update concentration and capability connects the local learning signal to training outcomes.
-
-We organize the paper around these three questions:
+We investigate this question through controlled comparisons with a Qwen3-1.7B student and four domain teachers covering mathematics, code, instruction following, and science. We combine algebraic analysis of loss averaging, local measurements at fixed student and optimizer states, and capability evaluations along training. Our contributions are threefold:
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{Token balancing (Section~\ref{sec:normalization}).}
-We derive how global-token, domain-token, and domain-response averaging weight domains and response lengths. The experiments show how these choices redistribute capability gains across domains.
-\item \textbf{Update sparsity and teacher overlap (Section~\ref{sec:subnetworks}).}
-We measure concentration of joint parameter changes, distinguishing individual steps from cumulative changes and optimizer weights from stored model weights. We formulate the teacher comparison at a shared student state and examine teacher-conditioned gradients in the appendix.
-\item \textbf{Supervision density (Section~\ref{sec:density}).}
-We compare sampled-token and top-$k$ supervision in single-teacher and joint training, and evaluate broader supervision at a common state. Local update concentration and online capability provide complementary measurements of these losses.
+\item \textbf{Separating domain balancing from response weighting (Section~\ref{sec:normalization}).}
+We distinguish global-token, domain-token, and domain-response averaging and show that within-domain length weighting changes gradient direction even when domain weights are identical. At the common update-50 comparison, global-token averaging achieves the highest mean score, while domain-response averaging yields the smallest worst-domain decline from initialization. The choice therefore depends on the intended capability tradeoff.
+\item \textbf{Characterizing parameter changes across numerical representations (Section~\ref{sec:subnetworks}).}
+We measure individual optimizer steps and cumulative changes separately in FP32 optimizer weights and BF16 model weights. At update 250, cumulative changes in the two joint-training branches are nonzero in 5.9--7.1\% of BF16 coordinates and 95.7--96.1\% of FP32 coordinates. These measurements clarify how sparse stored changes coexist with broad optimization activity in MOPD.
+\item \textbf{Comparing vocabulary supervision through both updates and capability (Section~\ref{sec:density}).}
+At the tested shared state, sampled-token, top-$k$, and full-vocabulary losses produce similar local update concentration. Online training nevertheless yields different capability profiles: in four-teacher training at update 250, student--teacher top-64 intersection (I64) supervision scores higher than sampled-token supervision in three domains and ties on instruction following. The mathematics-only teacher setting retains cross-domain tradeoffs, motivating evaluation beyond local sparsity alone.
 \end{enumerate}
 
-Section~\ref{sec:recipe} brings these studies together in a practical training recipe, connecting supervision choices to per-domain capability retention and specifying the validation still needed.
+We use these findings to outline a candidate MOPD recipe combining explicit supervision weights, vocabulary feedback selected using capability evaluations, and checkpoint selection under per-domain retention constraints (Section~\ref{sec:recipe}). Its combined effectiveness and stability remain to be validated.
